## The screens the campaign adds

`specs/ui.md` defines `title`, `howto`, and `playing`. Three more screens carry
the course, and `state.screen` names the one the game is in. `select` and
`complete` are reached in the campaign alone. `solved` is reached whichever way
the game is being played, and the choices it offers below are the campaign's.

| Screen | What it shows | How it is reached | How it is left |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `select` | The grid of all `24` boards, each showing its number and whether it is locked, unlocked, or solved. | Choosing `CAMPAIGN` on the title menu, and from `playing`, `solved`, and `complete`. | Entering an unlocked board goes to `playing`. `back` returns to `title`. |
| `solved` | The finished board, with the choices below drawn over it. | A board becomes solved, and boards remain unsolved. | A choice is taken: `playing` for the next board or a replay, `select` for the grid. |
| `complete` | Confirmation that all `24` boards are solved. | The solve that leaves no board unsolved. | A choice is taken: `select` for the grid, `title` for the main menu. |

Every menu on these screens is driven by the registered actions `up`, `down`,
`left`, `right`, `confirm`, and `back`, which `specs/controls.md` defines.

## The select screen

- The grid presents all `24` boards in number order, six columns wide and four
  rows tall, one row per set, Set A at the top. Each row is labelled with its
  set's entry in `SET_LABELS` (`SET A`, `SET B`, `SET C`, `SET D`), in that
  order from the top row down.
- Each board in the grid shows its number and reads, at a glance, as one of three
  states:

  | State | Meaning |
  | --- | --- |
  | locked | Not yet reached. Cannot be entered. |
  | unlocked | Reached and not solved. Can be entered. |
  | solved | Solved at least once. Can be entered again. |

- One board in the grid is highlighted, drawn distinctly from the rest. On
  arriving at the screen the highlight sits on the board the player most recently
  entered or solved, so a solve is seen landing on the grid. Before any board has
  been entered it sits on board `1`.
- `left` and `right` move the highlight within its row, wrapping from either end
  of the row to the other. `up` and `down` move it between rows in the same
  column, wrapping between the top row and the bottom row.
- `confirm` on an unlocked or solved board enters it and goes to `playing`.
  `confirm` on a locked board does nothing and leaves the highlight where it is.
- `back` returns to `title`.

The three board states read without relying on hue alone, since none of them
carries a channel's hue. Presentation is otherwise the build's to design, within
the legibility requirements in `specs/overview.md`.

## The solved screen

- On the frame a board satisfies rule R9 in `specs/beams.md`, play on that board
  ends and the game moves to `solved`.
- A trace live on that frame ends with it, and the beams stay exactly as drawn, so
  the screen is entered with no trace live. The release edge that follows begins
  nothing and changes nothing, and the solved board cannot be taken apart again
  from this screen.
- The finished board stays visible behind the screen, with every beam as the
  player drew it. The screen is drawn over it and does not hide it.
- The screen names the board that was solved and offers a short vertical menu of
  onward choices, in this order:

  | Choice | Offered when | Does |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | next board | The solved board is not board `24` | Enters board `n + 1` and goes to `playing`. |
  | replay | Always | Enters the same board again, with every beam empty, and goes to `playing`. |
  | back to select | Always | Goes to `select`. |

- The first choice is highlighted on arriving at the screen. `up` and `down` move
  the highlight, wrapping at both ends, `confirm` takes the highlighted choice,
  and `back` goes to `select`.
- The wording of the three choices is the build's own. Their order and their
  effects are not.

## The complete screen

- When a solve leaves no board unsolved, the game goes to `complete` instead of
  `solved`. It is the campaign's ending, and it is entered the same way `solved`
  is: the trace ends with the solve and the board is left exactly as drawn.
- The screen states that all `CAMPAIGN_LENGTH` (`24`) boards are solved.
- It offers two choices, in this order: back to `select`, and back to `title`. The
  first is highlighted on arriving at the screen, `up` and `down` move the
  highlight, wrapping at both ends, `confirm` takes it, and `back` goes to
  `select`.
- Every board stays unlocked and solved afterward, so the player can return to the
  grid and replay any of them.

## Leaving a board

- `back` during `playing` returns to `select`. The beams drawn on the board are
  discarded, and the board is unchanged the next time it is entered.
- The `clear` action empties every beam on the current board and leaves the player
  on it. `specs/controls.md` defines it, along with the pointer input a trace runs
  on.
- No board is in play on `select`, `solved`, or `complete`, so `clear` changes
  nothing on those three screens.
